\h Luke
\toc1 Luke
\toc2 Luke
\toc3 Luke
\mt2 The first account by
\mt1 Luke
\mt2 being the account of Yeshua's ministry
\is1 Introduction
\is2 Author
\ip This account about the works and teachings of Yeshua was written by Luke, one of the later followers of Yeshua. Luke was believed to be a medical doctor and although many have assumed him to be a Greek, there's also a good case that he was Jewish. He was good friends with Paul and accompanied him several times as they travelled to various places to spread the good message about Yeshua the messiah. Luke was also the author of the account of the beginnings of the Christian church known commonly known as \bk Acts\bk*, and unlike most other Bibles, the \em Open English Translation\em* places these two successive accounts one after the other.
\is2 This account
\ip This account was written to Theophilus who is said to be an honoured person from Rome. He starts his account with the prophesied birth of Yohan-the-immerser and then of Yeshua right through until he's lifted into the clouds. Luke also explains how that Yeshua is the messiah promised by God to save Jews and all non-Jews who put their trust in him.
\ip It's also told here in this document about Yeshua's mercy towards troubled people and sinners, his healing of many who were sick or possessed by demons, his bringing dead people back to life, and his teaching. Recurring themes here include God's spirit, prayer, forgiveness of sins, the women who helped Yeshua with his work, and how believers should prepare for his return.
\iot Main components of Luke's first account
\io1 Introduction \ior 1:1-4\ior*
\io1 The birth and childhood of Yohan-the-immerser and of Yeshua \ior 1:5–2:52\ior*
\io1 The ministry of Yohan-the-immerser \ior 3:1-20\ior*
\io1 The immersion and testing of Yeshua \ior 3:21–4:13\ior*
\io1 Yeshua's ministry up in Galilee \ior 4:14–9:50\ior*
\io1 Moving from Galilee towards Yerushalem \ior 9:51–19:27\ior*
\io1 The final week in and near Yerushalem \ior 19:28–23:56\ior*
\io1 The resurrection, appearances, and exit of Yeshua \ior 24:1-53\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\ie
\c 1
\s1 Introduction
\rem /s1 Dedication to Theophilus
\p
\v 1 Several people attempted¦37265 to¦37266 write an account about the¦37270 events that we're personally certain of
\v 2 as¦37277 the ones who were eyewitnesses¦37283 and¦37284 hearers of these accounts from the beginning¦37282 handed them¦37278 over¦37278 to¦37279 us¦37279.
\v 3 So since we had¦37292 followed¦37292 all¦37294 these events so carefully, it¦37290 seemed like a good idea to write¦37298 this account for you¦37297, honoured Theophilus¦37300,
\v 4 so that¦37305 you could¦37302 know¦37302 the definite background behind what you were¦37306 taught.
\s1 The messenger foretells the birth of Yohan-the-immerser
\rem /s1 The Birth of John the Baptist Is Announced; The Birth of John the Baptist Foretold; The coming of Christ
\p
\v 5 It started with the priest¦37319 Zekaryah \add !(Zechariah¦37322)\add* and his wife¦37328 Elizabeth at the time¦37313 when Herod¦37314 was the king¦37316 of Yudea¦37318. (Zekaryah was in the priestly division¦37324 of Abiyah, and Elizabeth was also a descendant of Aaron¦37334.)\x + \xo 1:5: \xt 1Ch 24:10.\x*
\v 6 \nd God¦37348\nd* considered them both¦37344 good and blameless¦37358 as they¦37341 followed his instructions and obeyed his rules.
\v 7 Elizabeth¦37367 had never been able to¦37362 have a child¦37363, so there was¦37368 just the¦37375 two of¦37377 them¦37377 as they were¦37378 now getting older.
\p
\v 8 One time¦37382 as he was taking his rostered turn to¦37383 serve \nd God¦37394\nd* along with his division¦37389,
\v 9 he was chosen¦37400 by¦37400 lot¦37400 (this was how the priests¦37399 assigned tasks) to¦37403 light the incense¦37403 in the temple¦37407, and so he proceeded inside.
\v 10 A lot of¦37416 people¦37417 would be praying¦37419 outside¦37420 the temple at¦37421 the time when the incense¦37424 was burnt.
\v 11 At that moment, a messenger¦37428 from God appeared¦37425 to¦37427 Zekaryah, standing¦37430 right there at the right of the incense¦37436 altar¦37434.
\v 12 Zekaryah¦37439 got a big fright at seeing¦37440 him¦37445 there and¦37437 started to tremble.
\v 13 But¦37462 the messenger¦37452 spoke, “Don't¦37453 be scared Zekaryah, because¦37457 your request has been listened¦37458 to, and your wife¦37464 Elizabeth¦37466 will¦37468 give birth to a son¦37469 and you'll name¦37475 him¦37476 Yohan¦37477.
\v 14 He'll bring a lot of happiness to¦37481 you and¦37478 you'll be proud of him¦37491—in fact many¦37486 people will¦37492 be glad.
\v 15 He'll be considered special in God's eyes, but¦37501 he won't¦37506 be allowed to drink¦37507 wine¦37502 or spirits. Even while he's still¦37512 in his¦37518 mother's¦37517 womb¦37515, he'll be filled¦37511 with God's \nd spirit¦37509\nd*.\x + \xo 1:15: \xt Num 6:3.\x*
\v 16 He¦37524 will¦37524 turn¦37524 many¦37520 children of Yisrael¦37523 back to¦37525 \nd Yahweh¦37526\nd*, their¦37529 \nd God¦37528\nd*.
\v 17 And he¦37531 will¦37533 go¦37533 on ahead¦37533 of God with the spirit¦37537 and the power¦37539 of Eliyah, to turn fathers¦37544 back¦37542 to loving their children¦37546 and to turn career criminals towards the wisdom of godly¦37551 people, to get the people ready for \nd Yahweh¦37554\nd*.”\x + \xo 1:17: \xt Mal 4:5-6.\x*
\p
\v 18 Zekaryah asked the messenger¦37562, “How will I know¦37565 if \add ≈you're telling the truth\add*, because¦37568 I'm an older¦37570 man¦37570 and¦37571 my¦37574 wife¦37573 is also getting old?”
\p
\v 19 Now the¦37582 messenger¦37583 rebuked him¦37585, “I am¦37587 Gabriel¦37588 who stood¦37590 right in front of \nd God¦37593\nd* and was¦37595 commanded to come here and talk to you, and to tell all of this to you,\x + \xo 1:19: \xt Dan 8:16; 9:21.\x*
\v 20 so listen to this: Because¦37617 you didn't¦37608 believe¦37620 my¦37623 message¦37622, you'll be¦37605 made silent¦37606 and unable to speak¦37610 until¦37611 the day¦37613 that¦37624 all of¦37623 these¦37616 things happen, and they certainly will¦37625 happen at the proper times.”
\p
\v 21 Meanwhile, the people¦37634 outside were expecting¦37636 Zekaryah to¦37644 come back out, and¦37639 were wondering what could be¦37644 holding him¦37645 up there inside the temple¦37648.
\v 22 But¦37664 when he did emerge, he wasn't able¦37653 to speak¦37654 to them¦37655. They¦37657 wondering if maybe he'd seen¦37660 a vision¦37659 in¦37661 the¦37662 temple¦37663, and¦37664 he nodded to confirm this although he remained mute¦37672,
\v 23 and¦37673 after he'd finished his¦37681 rostered days¦37678 in the temple, he returned home¦37686.
\p
\v 24 As time¦37694 went on, Elizabeth¦37697 became pregnant and¦37701 secluded¦37702 herself¦37703 away for five¦37705 months¦37704, saying¦37706,
\v 25 “\nd Yahweh¦37712\nd* has¦37710 done¦37710 this for me. At this time¦37714 he's¦37716 looked¦37716 down at me and taken¦37717 away¦37717 the¦37719 public shame of¦37720 my¦37720 being childless.”
\s1 God's messenger comes to Maria
